    "readme_description": "Strings `s1` and `s2` are `k`-**similar** (for some non-negative integer `k`) if we can swap the positions of two letters in `s1` exactly `k` times so that the resulting string equals `s2`.\n\nGiven two anagrams `s1` and `s2`, return the smallest `k` for which `s1` and `s2` are `k`-**similar**.",
    "_readme_examples": {
        "list": [
            {
                "content": "```\nInput: s1 = \"ab\", s2 = \"ba\"\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: The two strings are 1-similar because we can use one swap to change s1 to s2: \"ab\" --> \"ba\".\n```"
            },
            {
                "content": "```\nInput: s1 = \"abc\", s2 = \"bca\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: The two strings are 2-similar because we can use two swaps to change s1 to s2: \"abc\" --> \"bac\" --> \"bca\".\n```"
            }
        ]
